<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxyrhynchus hymn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">papyrus from around the end of the 3rd century AD</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> hymn, catalogued as P. Oxy. XV 1786, is the earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of a Christian hymn in Greek that preserves both its words and its musical notation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> dates from around the end of the 3rd century CE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was discovered in 1918 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and published in 1922. It is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> 1786 of the Oxyrhynchus papyri and was kept at the Papyrology Rooms of the Sackler Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, renamed the Bodleian Art, Archaeology and Ancient World Library in 2022. It is the only surviving fragment of notated Christian Greek music from the first four hundred years of the Christian period, although the historian and musician Kenneth Levy has argued that the Sanctus melody best preserved in the Western medieval Requiem mass dates from around the fourth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Greek words call for silence so that the Father, Son and Holy Spirit may be praised, drawing on cosmic stillness, a motif of ancient Greek hymnody. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron</a>, the hymn is among the earliest Christian Greek hymn texts reasonably certain to have been used in worship that neither come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> nor are modelled on biblical passages. It shows educated Christian Greeks using the musical notation of their classical predecessors.</p><p>The music is written in Greek vocal notation. It is entirely diatonic, spans exactly an octave from F to the F above, and ends nominally on G. The notation is Hypolydian and uses the rhythmic signs macron, leimma with macron, stigme, hyphen and colon. The text is set mostly one note to a syllable, with a few short melismas, and the metre is essentially anapaestic, with some irregularities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The hymn has been recorded on several albums of ancient Greek music: by Atrium Musicæ de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> under Gregorio Paniagua in 1979, by Christodoulos Halaris in 1992, by Ensemble De Organographia in 1995 and by Ensemble Kérylos, directed by the scholar Annie Bélis, in 1996. In 2025 it was adapted into a contemporary worship song for The First Hymn, a documentary project led by the Australian historian John Dickson, with an arrangement by Chris Tomlin and Ben Fielding that appeared on Tomlin's album The King Is Still the King.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oxyrhynchus hymn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
